node_modules/
dist/
.vite/
*.local
.DS_Store

# Derived images. `scripts/encode-assets.mjs` re-encodes every master in
# public/assets to WebP, and `vite.config.ts` ships those instead of the masters
# — 291 MB of public assets becomes 16 MB in dist. This folder is that output
# plus the content-hash index that makes the encode incremental.
#
# Not committed, on purpose. It is derivable from files that ARE committed, and
# committing it would put a second copy of all 296 paintings in a repository
# that already holds the masters — the exact duplication the encode step exists
# to keep out of dist. CI restores it from actions/cache instead (see
# .github/workflows/deploy.yml); a cold cache costs about 80 seconds.
.asset-cache/

# Build output of the verify:* scripts. Regenerated on every run, 60 MB of PNGs,
# and never an input to anything — the scripts assert against the live DOM and
# save these only so a human can look at what failed.
scripts/screenshots/

# Reference material, not source: 458 MB of frames extracted from a Hearthstone
# capture, used to study card framing and animation timing. Referenced by no
# code, and it is Blizzard's art — it must not end up in a public repository.
hearthstone_frames/

# Cloudflare Workers. `.dev.vars` holds local secrets — currently just the
# ADMIN_TOKEN guarding match creation. Notably it does NOT hold a Supabase
# service key: tokens are verified against the project's public JWKS, so the
# server has no credential that could read a table or forge an identity.
# `.dev.vars.example` is committed and documents both.
.wrangler/
.dev.vars

# Hand-kept notes of account and access details. The repository is public, so
# anything here would be published the moment it was staged — including by a
# `git add -A`, which is how this project has been committing.
#
# Ignoring a file is a seatbelt, not a lock: `git add -f` overrides it, archives
# and backups do not read it, and a rename to something not listed here is not
# covered. Real credentials are safer outside the working tree entirely — a
# password manager, or `wrangler secret put`, which stores them at Cloudflare
# and never on disk.
server access.txt
access.txt
credentials.txt
secrets.txt
*.secrets
